# FEATURE-DOCTOR-TRUSTED-ANDROID-DEVICE-LOCK-1 Phase 3 — durable rules

Phase 3 builds the Android Clinic App and the cryptographic device channel.
Rules 138 (Phase 1 room scope + print denial) and 139 (Phase 2 device registry)
stay fully in force and must not be weakened.

## 1. Enforcement is STILL off — and structurally so

Nothing in authentication, session handling or web middleware may reference
`DoctorDevice`, and no web route may carry a device middleware.
`DoctorDeviceApiAndNoEnforcementTest` asserts both: a source scan of the auth
surfaces, and a sweep of every registered route's middleware.

A passing "a doctor can still log in" test does NOT prove this — a coupling that
has not fired yet still passes. If you are about to add that reference, you are
starting Phase 4/5; get the decision made first.

## 2. Device identity ≠ user session

A successful proof means exactly **"this request came from registered clinic
hardware"**. It does not authenticate a Doctor, and the API says so in its own
response body. Merging the two concepts is Phase 4/5 work with its own review.

## 3. Private key custody

The keypair is generated **inside the Android Keystore** and the private key is
non-exportable. It is never transmitted, never written to SharedPreferences,
never handed to the WebView, and never logged. There is deliberately no
`exportPrivateKey` anywhere — `DeviceIdentityManager.signChallenge()` uses the
key by reference and returns only a signature.

StrongBox is requested **opportunistically** and falls back to the TEE-backed
Keystore: many legitimate clinic tablets have no StrongBox, and a hard
requirement would refuse acceptable hardware.

Clearing app data destroys the identity — intended. That is also why copying the
APK to another device grants nothing: the new install generates a different key
that no administrator has approved.

## 4. Never weak device identity

MAC address, IMEI, Android ID, serial, User-Agent, a hidden HTTP field, a cookie
or an installation UUID are **never** trust authority — alone or combined.
Possession of the Keystore private key is the only authority. Claimed device
metadata (platform/model/os/app version) is advisory context for the approving
administrator and nothing more.

## 5. The canonical signed message

    daengtisiams-device-proof|v1|<purpose>|<nonce>|<fingerprint>

Fixed, pipe-delimited and versioned — never JSON, whose key ordering and
whitespace are not guaranteed across platforms. `DeviceProofMessage` exists in
BOTH the server (`app/Modules/DoctorDevice/Support`) and the app
(`identity/DeviceProofMessage.kt`) and they must stay byte-identical. Changing
the format is a protocol version bump on both sides, not an edit.

It binds purpose + nonce + fingerprint, so a signature cannot be replayed across
devices or across purposes.

## 6. Challenge/response invariants

- Nonce: 32 CSPRNG bytes, unique, short-lived, bound to ONE device.
- **The nonce is burned in its own committed transaction BEFORE verification.**
  Doing the burn and the verification in one transaction is a real
  vulnerability: a denial throws, the transaction rolls back, the burn is undone
  and the attacker may retry signatures against a fixed nonce forever. A test
  pins this. Keep the two phases separate.
- Every denial returns the SAME opaque error; the reason lives only in the audit
  trail, so the endpoint cannot enumerate the device estate.
- Verification is delegated to OpenSSL. Never hand-roll a primitive.

## 7. Administrative status outranks cryptography

A proof succeeds only when ALL hold: registered · key bound · challenge is for
that device · challenge unexpired and unconsumed · signature verifies · status
is ACTIVE. Status is checked before issuing a challenge, after the signature
check, and again under lock before the write. REVOKED stays terminal.

## 8. Secure WebView

HTTPS only; the clinic host (or a subdomain) only; cleartext disabled in the
manifest **and** the network security config; `onReceivedSslError` **always**
cancels — never `proceed()`, not even in debug; file/content/universal file
access off; no `addJavascriptInterface` anywhere; WebView debugging enabled only
in debug builds. External links are **blocked, not handed to Chrome** — a
dedicated clinic device is not a browsing device.

`UrlAllowlist` is pure Kotlin so it is unit-tested directly, including the
suffix-match trap (`notdaengtisia.online`) and the userinfo trick
(`https://daengtisia.online@evil.com`).

## 9. Kiosk is Device Owner, not screen pinning

Screen pinning is user-dismissible and is not a security control. Lock Task
under Device Owner is the mechanism. Not-provisioned is a **supported** state:
the app still runs, it simply does not lock, and it reports that honestly rather
than implying protection it lacks. No hard-coded exit PIN and no secret gesture
— exit is a management action.

## 10. Doctor print denial is not bypassable from the app

The app adds no native print/PDF path for a Doctor. Phase 1's server-side denial
remains the authority; the WebView simply receives it.

## 11. Production signing — SUPERSEDED by rule 141

> **Phase 3.5 resolved this.** Custody, backup, rotation, distribution and
> disaster recovery are decided in
> `docs/adr/0009-android-production-signing-distribution-and-device-management.md`
> and enforced by `.cursor/rules/141-android-production-release-governance.mdc`.
> Read 141 for the current rule.

What survives unchanged: no production signing identity has been **created**,
and none may be invented. Release builds declare **no** `signingConfig` and must
never fall back to the debug key. Debug/test artifacts are for emulator and CI
only and must never be described as a production release. Deciding how to sign
is not signing — the key itself is a Phase 4 input.

## 12. Real-device validation is a Phase 4 blocker

Emulator validation does not prove hardware-backed Keystore, real Device Owner
provisioning on clinic hardware, or field behaviour. Never describe emulator
results as real-device validation.

## 13. Source-scanning guards must be comment-aware

Several controls here are enforced by scanning Kotlin source for a forbidden
call, because the Android framework classes involved have no meaningful JVM
substitute. A plain substring scan matches the comment that explains why the
call is forbidden — the prose is byte-identical to the code — so the guard goes
red on a codebase that already obeys it. That trains people to delete the guard.

Strip comments (string- and char-literal aware; Kotlin block comments nest)
before matching, and keep a presence assertion alongside every absence assertion
so a stripper that removes too much fails loudly instead of passing vacuously.

## 14. The Gradle wrapper is committed executable

Git records only mode 100644 or 100755. `android/daengtisia-clinic/gradlew` must
be 100755 **in the index**; a wrapper that is executable only on a developer's
disk checks out non-executable in CI and the gate dies at exit 126 before a
single test runs. Assert the index mode, never the working tree.

Verify Android changes from a clean extraction of the staged tree, not from the
working tree — one gate failure readily hides another.
